### BookVerse — the composed book page (Lesson 06).
### GET /books/:id/page joins a book with its reviews at read time.
###
### The two dependencies are NOT symmetric:
###   book-service    REQUIRED -- no book, no page
###   review-service  OPTIONAL -- the page renders without it, marked unavailable
###
### Every expectation below was measured, not assumed. Timings are from a warm
### local stack; what matters is the ORDER of magnitude, not the digits.
### Requires the "REST Client" VS Code extension.

@baseUrl = http://localhost:3000
@email = amr@example.com
@password = password123

### 1. Log in — only needed to create the fixtures below
# @name login
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

###
@accessToken = {{login.response.body.data.tokens.accessToken}}

### 2. A book that will have a review
# @name reviewedBook
POST {{baseUrl}}/books
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: page-reviewed-book

{
  "title": "Composed Page Fixture",
  "description": "Has at least one review.",
  "genre": ["test"]
}

###
@reviewedBookId = {{reviewedBook.response.body.data.id}}

### 3. Give it a review (409 on a re-run is fine — one review per user per book)
POST {{baseUrl}}/reviews
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "bookId": "{{reviewedBookId}}",
  "rating": 5,
  "comment": "The join has to live somewhere."
}

### 4. A book nobody has reviewed — the other half of the pair in row 2
# @name emptyBook
POST {{baseUrl}}/books
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: page-empty-book

{
  "title": "Nobody Has Reviewed This",
  "description": "Zero reviews, on purpose.",
  "genre": ["test"]
}

###
@emptyBookId = {{emptyBook.response.body.data.id}}

### ---------------------------------------------------------------
### THE HAPPY PATH
### ---------------------------------------------------------------

### 5. Row 1 — book + its reviews. → 200 in ~8ms
###    Both calls go out in parallel, so the page costs max(book, reviews),
###    not their sum. Compare against 6a and 6b below: each is ~8ms alone,
###    and the page is ~8ms, not ~16ms. That IS the parallelism test.
GET {{baseUrl}}/books/{{reviewedBookId}}/page

### 6a. The book hop alone, for the comparison above
GET {{baseUrl}}/books/{{reviewedBookId}}

### 6b. The reviews hop alone
GET {{baseUrl}}/reviews?bookId={{reviewedBookId}}

### 7. Row 2 — a book with ZERO reviews. → 200, reviews.status "ok", items []
###    THE PAIR THAT MATTERS: compare this against row 4 below. "No reviews
###    yet" and "we could not fetch the reviews" must not look the same. An
###    empty list is a fact; an unavailable list is an absence of facts.
GET {{baseUrl}}/books/{{emptyBookId}}/page

### 8. Row 6 — the book does not exist. → 404 in ~9ms
###    The 404 is passed through because it is the one upstream status that
###    is genuinely about the CLIENT's request. Everything else the callee
###    says maps to 502/503/504 -- see toClientStatus in bookDetailsHelpers.
GET {{baseUrl}}/books/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000/page

### ---------------------------------------------------------------
### DEGRADATION — the optional half fails, the page survives
### ---------------------------------------------------------------
###
### Row 4 — review-service STOPPED:
###   docker compose --env-file .env.dev stop review-service
###   then run 5 again.  Measured: 200 in 0.80s, reviews {"status":"unavailable"}
###
###   Note the 800ms. A stopped container HANGS rather than refusing, so the
###   page pays review's full timeout budget. "Down" and "slow" are the same
###   failure from here, and the reviews budget is what bounds both -- which
###   is why it is set BELOW the book budget. Restore with:
###   docker compose --env-file .env.dev start review-service
###
### Row 3 — review-service PAUSED (a true hang):
###   docker pause review-service   -> 200 in 0.81s, same shape
###   docker unpause review-service
###
### Row 5 — review-service's BREAKER OPEN (the interesting one):
###   Trip it through the proxy, 12 times:
###     for i in $(seq 1 12); do curl -s -o /dev/null \
###       -H "x-fault-status: 504" "http://localhost:3000/reviews?bookId=<id>"; done
###   Then run 5 again.  Measured: 200 in 0.019s -- degraded, and FAST.
###
###   That contrast is the whole point: 800ms when the service is merely down
###   (we open a socket and wait for the budget), 19ms when the breaker is
###   open (we never open a socket at all). Same visible outcome for the
###   reader, an order of magnitude less work.
###
###   Meanwhile the PROXY path on that same breaker refuses outright:
GET {{baseUrl}}/reviews?bookId={{reviewedBookId}}
### → 503 in ~4ms while the breaker is open. Same signal, two reactions:
###   a proxy has nothing to serve without its callee, a composer does.
###   The breaker closes itself ~50s after the last failure.

### ---------------------------------------------------------------
### THE REQUIRED HALF FAILS — the page must not survive
### ---------------------------------------------------------------
###
### Row 7 — book-service STOPPED:
###   docker compose --env-file .env.dev stop book-service
###   then run 5.  Measured: 504 in 1.01s -- the hang hit book's budget.
###   docker compose --env-file .env.dev start book-service
###
### Row 8 — book-service refusing connections outright:
###   docker pause book-service   -> 503 in 0.01s
###   docker unpause book-service
###
###   504 vs 503 is not arbitrary: one timed out, one was unreachable, and
###   the page reports which. Neither is ever 200-with-a-missing-book.
